# Bitbook

#PR#Notes#= **Bitbook** is a notebook with real pages. It uses the same Keydown engine as Writeout, plus a set of small **skills** (gadgets) and a programming language called [BitLang](#/bitlang) for little programs inside your notes!

[Open Bitbook](/bitbook) · [Phone version](/bitbook/mobile/new/)


::divider{wavy|Bitbook|green}
## The ribbon

| Tab | What you will find |
| --- | --- |
| #G.accent(Home) | Clipboard, Basic Text, Paragraph, Styles, Keydown Styles and Find |
| #TU.accent(Insert) | New Page, New Note, Table, File Attachment, Pictures, OpenDB Images, Inkpath drawings, 3D Scene, Image on Top, Link, Equation, Time Stamp, Symbol, To Do Tag and Bullets |
| #T.accent(Skills) | Chips (Uni-Chip, Custom Chip, Unique Styles, Custom CSS) and Gadgets (Jumper, BeepBox Song, Pixel Sprite, Custom Emoji, Time Capsule, Flashcard, Focus Timer, Dice Roller, Programs, Highlight, Annotate) |
| #C.accent(View) | View, Visual and Source modes, Pages View, Print Preview, Page Color, Rule Lines, Zoom and Layout |
| #B.accent(Help) | Help Deck and the BitLang Guide |

## Notes and pages

Notes are saved as `.kn` files. Like Writeout, a note can have several real pages (`::pagebreak`), a Pages view and a Print Preview. Writeout and Bitbook open each other's files!

## Skills

Skills turn a tag into a small working thing inside your text:

The #O.bucket(Skills) tab lists them under *Gadgets*: Jumper, BeepBox Song, Pixel Sprite, Custom Emoji, Time Capsule, Flashcard, Focus Timer, Dice Roller, Programs, Highlight and Annotate. Click one to insert it at the caret, and its tag appears in the text so you can edit it later in Source mode. #O.bucket(Chips) has the Uni-Chip, Custom Chip, Unique Styles and Custom CSS tools!

#O.bucket(Annotate) and #O.bucket(Highlight) mark up text with pens, which are written as Keydown tags. See [Annotator pens](#/keydown-start)!

## Programs

A BitLang program lives in a note. It can show text, make buttons, change how things look and react when you hover. The [BitLang guide](#/bitlang) has the language!

::tip Programs and editing
If you want a program to keep running while you read, stay in #TU.bucket(View) mode and do not type in the page while it runs. Typing rebuilds the page, which stops running programs!
::end
